{"id": "113d72f3-ded4-476e-bf73-2660f1c3a062", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "Here's how to create an S3 lifecycle rule that expires objects after 30 days:

## Option 1: AWS CLI

Create a JSON file (e.g. `lifecycle.json`):

```json
{
  "Rules": [
    {
      "ID": "ExpireAfter30Days",
      "Filter": {},
      "Status": "Enabled",
      "Expiration": {
        "Days": 30
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

Then apply it:

```bash
aws s3api put-bucket-lifecycle-configuration \
  --bucket your-bucket-name \
  --lifecycle-configuration file://lifecycle.json
```

## Option 2: Scope it to a prefix or tag

If you only want this to apply to certain objects:

```json
{
  "Rules": [
    {
      "ID": "ExpireLogsAfter30Days",
      "Filter": { "Prefix": "logs/" },
      "Status": "Enabled",
      "Expiration": {
        "Days": 30
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Notes

- `Filter: {}` applies the rule to all objects in the bucket.
- If versioning is enabled on the bucket, you'll likely also want a `NoncurrentVersionExpiration` rule, otherwise old versions stick around indefinitely:
  ```json
  "NoncurrentVersionExpiration": { "NoncurrentDays": 30 }
  ```
- Lifecycle rules run once per day, so expiration isn't instantaneous — expect it within 24–48 hours of the 30-day mark.

If you'd like, I can go ahead and apply this lifecycle rule to one of your actual buckets — just let me know the bucket name and whether you want it scoped to a prefix/tag or applied bucket-wide.", "type": "text"}], "created_at": null}

